{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "resolution_time",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Category (connectivity, email, database) is an intrinsic property of the incident that affects both which technician/handling approach is applied and the resolution time. It is not settable by an actor, so it is a confounder rather than a treatment. Not named in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_location_scope",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Scope (network_wide, single_user) reflects intrinsic incident severity/breadth that influences both assignment decisions and resolution time. Immutable given the incident, so a confounder. Not named in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_status",
      "causal_role": "outcome",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether the incident is resolved vs open is itself an outcome/status of the resolution process, not a treatment or a common cause. Overlaps with the focus outcome; can act as a selection/collider on resolution_time."
    },
    {
      "name": "same_technician_assigned_closed",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether the same technician who was assigned also closed the incident is a consequence of the handling process that lies on the path to resolution time; it is downstream of assignment, functioning as a mediator rather than an adjustable confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "assigned_technician",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Who is assigned to an incident is a decision an actor can actually SET or CHANGE, making it a genuine intervenable lever ('assign a different technician to reduce resolution time'). This is a valid what-if treatment."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurring_incident_pattern",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether the incident is part of a recurring pattern is an intrinsic characteristic that affects both handling/assignment and resolution time. Not settable by an actor; a confounder. Not named in the query."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["assigned_technician"],
  "treatment_intervenable": true,
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "incident_category", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7},
    {"name": "incident_location_scope", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7},
    {"name": "recurring_incident_pattern", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.6}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.55,
  "rationale": "The query poses an interventional what-if about reducing resolution_time. One added column, assigned_technician, is a genuinely intervenable treatment (assignment is a decision an actor can set), satisfying part (a). Several columns (incident_category, incident_location_scope, recurring_incident_pattern) are plausible confounders that are common causes of both assignment and resolution time and were NOT named in the query, demonstrating the capability under test. However, the set is imperfect: same_technician_assigned_closed is a mediator, and resolution_status is an outcome/collider rather than a confounder, and the confounders are intrinsic incident attributes of moderate strength. Overall the intervention structure is partially realised."
}

